7592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489129 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489129 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489129 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c645bc4d-e925-5887-895a-ddac573c52dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186488991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186488960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489156 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efd9e216-e266-5141-b8bb-3d95bb41fe42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $8\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2593d620-c288-5ff9-965a-23a30b035fd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489194 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/77/771929267e75b5d45b9777399c784fafc570386834eee6738fead9ff1bb78e5e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt[3]{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(0)^{4/3}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}{x}^{4/3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =\\frac{3k}{4}(8)^{4/3}+2 \\\\ 32 & =12k+2 \\\\ 12k & =30 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5208d74a-f4b7-5436-9bf1-918aa4bcaa25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489219 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =2(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+C \\\\ C & =6-16 \\\\ C & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}-10.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-10 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =2{x}^{2}-10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fda4f28-bb84-514e-9e1c-0feb53f5b922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489224 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/51/51ccc6e3e341931c7a244c4617ede78f901478487589f9023b16c3dae80ebc62.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=7-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $1>0$ | $0$ | $-1<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=7-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+6}=\\sqrt{55}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163580" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(2x-3)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $2\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $3\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf4a1221-96ce-539b-bfe1-3711516162e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489262 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/ca/ca6267f534b9cad9bc1646eef473d85aab1ba6a5f4738b8212b107b753ea2381.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x-3)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x-3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x-3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}-3x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=3\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(3)^{2}-3(3)+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}-3x+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,m,\\,2\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $k{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=8\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has a velocity of $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $4\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3dde6ce-080b-590e-80f0-4c60a929946f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489279 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f3/f3879ccc6084d9a0ef8bef2e211e2e7fd9dcc811fb04f06c2ef7429e4d574871.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $k{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-k{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-k{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-k{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=8\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8\\sqrt{17})^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =544\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=544-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=4\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =544-\\frac{k}{4}{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =544-\\frac{k}{4}(4)^{4} \\\\ 32 & =544-64k \\\\ 64k & =512 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(3+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=8\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $8\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f72c0da0-a5f4-5e3b-8c36-5827ed531170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489292 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f3/f368d2de12462a1790322edca188232bf8fafe3951facdb29b334e285807b9fe.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3+kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3+kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (3+kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =3(0)+\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =3(8)+\\frac{k(8)^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ 64 & =24+32k+32 \\\\ 32k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
